<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Transnistria</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Pridnestrovian Moldavian Republic, Pridnestrovie, Dniesteria, Transdniestria</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Transnistria is a landlocked breakaway state, internationally recognised as part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moldova">Moldova</a>, on the strip of land between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dniester">Dniester</a> river and the Moldova–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> border. Its origins lie in the Moldavian Autonomous Soviet Socialist Republic, formed in 1924 within the Ukrainian SSR. In 1990, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> dissolved, the Pridnestrovian Moldavian Soviet Socialist Republic was established. A military conflict with Moldova began in March 1992 and ended in a ceasefire in July that year. Its political status remains unresolved: it is unrecognised but de facto independent, with its own government, parliament and currency.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Transnistria is a narrow valley stretching north to south along the bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dniester">Dniester</a> river, which forms the natural boundary along most of its de facto border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moldova">Moldova</a>. It borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessarabia">Bessarabia</a>, the region on which the Republic of Moldova is based, for 411 kilometres to the west and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> for 405 kilometres to the east. Its capital and largest city is Tiraspol. It also controls the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bender-moldova">Bender</a> on the western bank, which the Moldovan authorities do not count as part of the territorial unit.</p><p>The name is the Romanian colloquial name of the region, meaning &quot;beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dniester">Dniester</a>&quot;. It was first used for eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moldova">Moldova</a> in 1989, in an election slogan of Leonida Lari, a member of the Popular Front of Moldova.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Moldavian Autonomous Soviet Socialist Republic was proclaimed in 1924 within the Ukrainian SSR. It included present-day Transnistria and an area around Balta, but nothing from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessarabia">Bessarabia</a>, then part of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>; one reason for its creation was the Soviet wish to incorporate Bessarabia eventually. On 28 June 1940 the USSR annexed Bessarabia and Northern Bukovina under the Molotov-Ribbentrop Pact, and on 2 August 1940 the Supreme Soviet created the Moldavian SSR from part of the annexed territory and part of the former autonomous republic roughly equivalent to Transnistria.</p><p>Armed clashes began at Dubăsari in November 1990, with volunteers including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a> coming from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> to help the separatists. Concerted fighting began on 2 March 1992, and the Soviet 14th Guards Army entered at the final stage; about 700 people were killed. A ceasefire was signed on 21 July 1992 and created a Joint Control Commission with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moldova">Moldova</a>, Russia and Transnistria and a trilateral peacekeeping force.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Transnistria is a semi-presidential republic with its own government, parliament, military, police, postal system and currency, the Transnistrian ruble, and its authorities have adopted a constitution, flag, national anthem and coat of arms. All UN member states treat it as part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moldova">Moldova</a>; only Abkhazia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-ossetia">South Ossetia</a> have recognised it. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> held by its authorities in 2015 counted 475,373 people, among them Russians (29.1 per cent), Moldovans (28.6) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukrainians">Ukrainians</a> (22.9), and the schools mainly use the Russian curriculum. In March 2022 the Parliamentary Assembly of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-europe">Council of Europe</a> adopted a resolution that defined the territory as under military occupation by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Transnistria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The economy was mainly agricultural, with most industry in <b>Transnistria</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/moldavian-ssr">Moldavian SSR</a></li><li><blockquote>A strip of territory on the east bank of the Dniester has been under the de facto control of the breakaway government of <b>Transnistria</b> since 1990.</blockquote><a href="/state/moldova">Moldova</a></li></ul></section>
